,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1758,2031-10-19,67,post_entry,early_collections,32.5072513385612850576044959606603420,1.78076159841925479452054794520547944,34.2880129369805398521250439058658214,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1759,2031-10-19,67,post_entry,operating_payment,34.2880129369805398521250439058658214,-1.62354245094217897260273972602739726,32.6644704860383608795223041798384241,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1760,2031-10-19,67,post_entry,late_collections,32.6644704860383608795223041798384241,0.197862399824361643835616438356164382,32.8623328858627225233579206181945885,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1761,2031-10-19,67,post_entry,cash_operating_tax,32.8623328858627225233579206181945885,-0.076178150054245027397260273972602740,32.7861547358084774959606603442219858,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1762,2031-10-19,67,post_entry,maintenance_cash_capex,32.7861547358084774959606603442219858,-0.0494907354580250,32.7366640003504524959606603442219858,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1763,2031-10-19,67,post_entry,cash_interest,32.7366640003504524959606603442219858,-0.0126760706533192834562697576396206535,32.7239879296971332125043905865823651,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1764,2031-10-19,67,post_entry,existing_term_principal,32.7239879296971332125043905865823651,0.00000,32.7239879296971332125043905865823651,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1765,2031-10-19,67,post_entry,solar_term_principal,32.7239879296971332125043905865823651,-0.0512820512820512820512820512820512821,32.6727058784150819304531085353003138,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1766,2031-10-19,68,post_entry,terminal_debt_repayment,32.6727058784150819304531085353003138,-7.04942307692307692307692307692307704,25.6232828014920050073761854583772368,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1767,2031-10-19,68,post_entry,legacy_long_gratuity_claim,25.6232828014920050073761854583772368,-0.4000,25.2232828014920050073761854583772368,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\np_4a64d910a045291750857611,1768,2031-10-19,69,post_entry,terminal_continuation_reserve_test,25.2232828014920050073761854583772368,0,25.2232828014920050073761854583772368,11067500,0.00000000000000000000000000000000000,0.1947,False,,,\\r\\n\"","\"# Roopa Screen Limited | BSE SME research\\n\\nInformation cutoff: 20 October 2026. Market reference: 19 October 2026. Framework: Indian Small/Micro-Cap Stock Research Master Prompt v3.0, dated 3 October 2026. All financial amounts are ₹ crore unless stated. This is completed analytical work with unresolved investment evidence, not a declaration that every missing fact has been verified.\\n\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**Hold: unresolved funding and execution evidence. BQS NS; GQS NS; IAS NS.** Roopa manufactures consumable rotary nickel screens for textile printing, alongside a growing nickel-cathode trading business. The opportunity is replacement demand plus an IPO-funded capacity expansion. The decisive favorable evidence is improved operating profit and utilization at the continuing Sanand factory; the decisive counterevidence is much slower manufactured-sales growth, weak recent cash realization, an old factory closure for missing approvals, and newly expanded debt. At the conditional ₹102 reference price, neither the base nor bull cash-linked diagnostic reaches the 15% equity hurdle. This is a capacity-expansion/cyclical manufacturing thesis, not a verified compounder. Actual post-IPO bank cash and utilization, sold-volume economics, solar-project spending, commissioning and executable liquidity could change the conclusion. Missing evidence is not proof of fraud or insolvency. [S01](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Roopa-Scree-Limited-Prospectus-2.pdf), E02–E15.\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\nLegal entity **Roopa Screen Limited**, previously Roopa Screen Private Limited; CIN U28112GJ2013PLC074471; **ISIN INE2BTZ01011**, BSE SME **544954 / ROOPA**. Do not confuse it with Roopa Industries. Incorporated 12 April 2013; public conversion 2 June 2025. The research uses standalone Indian GAAP Accounting Standards, not assumed Ind AS. No subsidiaries/JVs or outstanding convertible securities were identified in the reviewed prospectus. [S01, cover/capital/business; E01]\\n\\n₹102 is a secondary-reported BSE close on 19 October, corroborated by Anand Rathi and Trendlyne, not a verified executable bid. Post-IPO issued shares are 11,067,500; conditional market capitalization is **₹112.8885cr**. The primary listing PDF and BSE tape could not be retrieved. The dated NSE interop security master supports identity, share count and 2,000-share lot, but is not a BSE execution record. Horizon: five years to 19 October 2031. Equity hurdle: 15% nominal before personal taxes, with 12%/18% sensitivity. Illustrative position: ₹5 lakh, not a recommended allocation. [E01/E15; S16–S19]\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Resolution needed |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Obtain compliance closure, arm’s-length RPT terms, beneficial ownership and encumbrance verification. |\\n| G2 | Conditional | Reconcile original/restated cash flow, fixed-asset registers and borrowing-base statements. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | Obtain bank statements, drawdowns, purpose utilization, dated maturities, covenants and committed funding. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | Obtain primary traded values, missing/zero-session distinction, lot, spread, circuits and available free float. |\\n\\nG1 is Conditional, not evidenced abuse: family-controlled property/creditor exposure, a licensing failure and reporting differences need scrutiny. G2 is Conditional because later audits are unmodified with affirmative IFC, but the original-to-restated FY24 cash-flow bridge remains incomplete. G3/G4 are Hold, not demonstrated defaults or suspension. An assumed cash shortfall in a stress model does not establish actual issuer insolvency.\\n\\nConfidence is High on exact identity and disclosed capacity/IPO allocation; Medium on restated financials, original-audit interpretation, governance, capital-return proxies and dated debt; Low on constant-price organic demand, actual October available cash, achievable forecast returns and implementation. Overall investment confidence is Low, the weakest material area, not an average. Forecast uncertainty is separate from accurate extraction.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\n| Measure | Known K | Coverage | Mathematical raw bound | Final |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 4.04 | 88.00% | ['4.16', '5.24'] | NS |\\n| GQS | 3.30 | 60.00% | ['3.70', '7.30'] | NS |\\n| IAS | 0.00 | 0.00% | ['1.00', '10.00'] | NS |\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | E06,E08,E09,E11,E13 |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | E02,E03,E04,S11,S13,S15 |\\n| REV | NS | Low | E02,E03 |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | E03,E04,E05 |\\n| CAP | 6 | Medium | E10,E12 |\\n| CASH | 5 | Medium | E07,E12 |\\n| BAL | 5 | Medium | E10,E14 |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium | E03,E05,E06,commitment_outcomes.json |\\n| EARN | 6 | Medium | E02,E07,E12 |\\n| R | NS | Low | E14,E15 |\\n| D | NS | Low | E10,E14 |\\n| P | NS | Low | E02,E14,E15 |\\n| L | NS | Low | E15 |\\n\\n| Aggregate | Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | GOV | 0.20 | 4 | 0.80 |\\n| BQS | MOAT | 0.20 | 4 | 0.80 |\\n| BQS | REV | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | DUR | 0.10 | 5 | 0.50 |\\n| BQS | CAP | 0.12 | 6 | 0.72 |\\n| BQS | CASH | 0.10 | 5 | 0.50 |\\n| BQS | BAL | 0.08 | 5 | 0.40 |\\n| BQS | EXEC | 0.08 | 4 | 0.32 |\\n| GQS | REV | 0.40 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | DUR | 0.20 | 5 | 1.00 |\\n| GQS | EARN | 0.15 | 6 | 0.90 |\\n| GQS | CAP | 0.15 | 6 | 0.90 |\\n| GQS | CASH | 0.10 | 5 | 0.50 |\\n| IAS | BQS | 0.25 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | R | 0.35 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | D | 0.20 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | P | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | L | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n\\nAll 13 factors are assigned once and reused. GOV/MOAT/EXEC at 4 reflect documented weaknesses without alleging abuse; DUR/BAL/CASH at 5 reflect mixed evidence; CAP/EARN at 6 reflect historical improvement with important base/capital/cash qualifications. REV is NS because total sales, nickel trading and manufactured rupee sales do not establish constant-price underlying demand. The three-year window was chosen because a five-year comparable series was not located; we did not cherry-pick the faster two-year total-sales period.\\n\\nR is NS rather than mechanically scored 1 from an unsupported base forecast; D/P are NS because present funding and operating assumptions are not established; L is NS because required primary execution data are incomplete. Known-factor confidence is Medium and historical scores are capped at 8 under the short-history convention, although none approaches that ceiling.\\n\\nNo numeric overrides are applied: GOV is not ≤3, no evidenced G1 failure, no proven G3 failure, and one weak cash year does not establish persistent CASH≤3. NS stays NS, never a cap number. The generic IAS bound is [1,10]; propagating the BQS mathematical bound gives a narrower **[1.79,8.81]**, still not a point score or confidence interval. Moving uncertain GOV and CAP by one point together shifts BQS's known contribution by ±0.32 and GQS by ±0.15. No extra risk haircut, undisclosed composite or ranking is permitted. Exact Decimal arithmetic and half-up display rounding are in `score_audit.json`.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nElectroformed rotary nickel screens transfer repeating designs onto textiles. Standard, Delta, Penta and Nova product families vary in mesh/design; durability, uniformity and print quality matter to processors. Screens are recurring consumables, but demand depends on customers' textile throughput and the competition between rotary and digital printing. Nickel cathodes are also traded without material value addition. Trading revenue is not equivalent to manufactured screen demand or proprietary technology. [S01 business/Annex21; E02]\\n\\nFY26 manufacturing contributed ₹42.0734cr, trading ₹8.5650cr and freight ₹0.0888cr. Domestic sales were 99.69%, Gujarat 58.93% and the top five states 96.58%. The top ten customers represented 34.53%, compared with 27.22% in FY25; customers fell to 210 from 228. Top-ten suppliers supplied 96.73%. Penta contributed 38.54% of FY26 sales. Named counterparty disclosures do not demonstrate long-term contracts; firm purchase commitments were not established. Receivables are unsecured; 122.76 lakh was older than six months at March 2026 versus 78.82 lakh a year earlier. [S01 customer/supplier/geography tables, Annex18; E04]\\n\\nThe continuing Sanand unit has 74,400 annual-screen capacity and produced 71,800 in FY26, 96.51% utilization. The closed Narol unit produced another 6,800 before its 15 December 2025 closure. Its 14,400 historical capacity must not be counted as continuing capacity or added again to the new project. Total produced units, 78,600, are not units sold. [S01 manufacturing/risks; E03]\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\nA plausible advantage is precision electroforming, consistent mesh and customer qualification/service. It is not yet an evidenced strong moat: verified customer retention, lifetime cost advantage, rejection rates, sustained price premium, proprietary IP and independent market-share gains are missing. Product-family names and high utilization alone do not prove pricing power. Nickel volatility, concentrated procurement, textile customer bargaining and substitution by digital printing can weaken economics.\\n\\nThree named primary economic peers challenge the thesis:\\n\\n- **Stovec Industries** is a listed rotary-screen, textile-machinery and digital/graphic-solutions competitor with broader technology and product scope. CY2025 audited revenue fell to ₹198.111cr from ₹234.570cr; PAT fell to ₹6.898cr from ₹12.961cr; CFO was negative ₹1.929cr versus positive ₹16.186cr. Operating EBIT excluding all other income is ₹4.009cr and EBITDA ₹9.765cr; the annual-report highlight uses a different EBITDA definition. These definitions are not mixed. A stronger technology lineage does not guarantee profitable sector growth or cash conversion. Its H1 2026 limited-reviewed report was also read, not annualized. No verified cutoff peer price was obtained, so there is no transplanted current P/E. [S11](https://www.spgprints.com/hubfs/Annaul%20Report_2025.pdf?hsLang=en), [S12](https://www.spgprints.com/hubfs/Results%202nd%20Quarter%202026.pdf?hsLang=en).\\n- **Screen-O-Tex India Private Limited** is a direct rotary-screen/processed-nickel-sheet competitor. Its September 2024 Infomerics rationale cites FY23 revenue ₹76.31cr, EBITDA ₹4.73cr and PAT ₹1.03cr. The historical working-capital cycle was 136 days; facility utilization was 92.6% in July 2024 and March 2024 cash only ₹0.08cr. The historical BB-/Stable/A4 assessment is not represented as its current rating. Promoter quasi-equity treatment also makes credit leverage non-comparable without adjustment. This is contrary evidence against treating screen manufacture as automatically cash-rich. [S15](https://infomericstorage.blob.core.windows.net/uploads/pr_Screen_Tex_4sep24_fc49817e21.pdf), [S14](https://screenotexindia.com/quality/).\\n- **Gurjar Group** markets directly competing precision rotary screens and fine meshes. Its official product pages support competing mechanisms, not independently verified margins or market share. Private peer financials/current valuation remain ND. SPGPrints is Stovec's parent, not a fourth independent competitor. [S13](https://www.gurjargroup.com/delta.php).\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\n| Interval | Total sales CAGR | Manufactured CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23–FY26, 3 years | 13.53% | 7.47% |\\n| FY24–FY26, 2 years | 19.19% | 9.35% |\\n| 5 / 10 years | ND | ND |\\n\\n| Year | Sales addition | Sales YoY | Manufactured YoY |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY24 | 1.04 | 3.01% | 3.82% |\\n| FY25 | 9.64 | 26.99% | 13.38% |\\n| FY26 | 5.39 | 11.88% | 5.45% |\\n\\nFY23/FY24/FY25/FY26 total revenue was ₹34.6628/35.7053/45.3412/50.7273cr; manufactured revenue ₹33.8943/35.1890/39.8975/42.0734cr. The FY25 addition was disproportionately helped by trading, which rose from ₹0.4596cr to ₹5.3637cr and then ₹8.5650cr. FY26 manufactured-sales growth was only about 5.45% versus 11.88% total-sales growth. [S01 Annex21, S02 Annex20; E02]\\n\\nManufacturing's two-year CAGR is approximately 9.35%, versus approximately 19.20% reported sales. Total output was 73,078/71,127/78,600 screens for FY24/25/26, while the continuing Sanand unit produced 62,038/60,214/71,800. These are production, not invoiced volumes; inventory moved substantially. Dividing revenue by output is a rough plausibility check, not realized selling price. No acquisition-driven growth was identified; price/mix, nickel pass-through and the closure/relocation remain unresolved demand decompositions. Exports were only 0.31% in FY26. Five-/ten-year CAGR and comparable post-FY26 TTM growth are ND, not zero. No single quarter was annualized.\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nThe proposed adjacent-site addition of 88,800 screens would raise capacity to 163,200, more than double the continuing base. It is planned, not operating. The ₹10.6098cr project consists of civil work ₹2.9757cr, plant ₹6.7188cr, electrical ₹0.4101cr and contingency ₹0.5052cr. A ₹0.20cr construction cheque is disclosed on 2 July 2026. IPO allocation is ₹9.9046cr with remaining contingency internally funded. The schedule envisages October 2026 ordering, January delivery, February installation/trial and March 2027 commercial production. There was no bank appraisal. [S01 objects; S10; E05]\\n\\nIPO gross proceeds of ₹19.20cr less expenses of ₹3.2773cr yield ₹15.9227cr: project ₹9.9046cr, working capital ₹6.00cr and general corporate purpose ₹0.0181cr. Expense intensity is about 17.07% of gross. March prepaid IPO expense ₹0.5189cr is already outside cash; only the remaining ₹2.7584cr fee is deducted on modeled receipt. The project reserve is ring-fenced and cannot fund ordinary operations, and earmarked working capital is not a dividend. Management's internal-accrual estimates are not bank balances. [S01 objects and Annex28]\\n\\nThe bottom-up model assumes sold-unit bases, 3%/12%/18% growth, explicit unit prices and a 98% capacity ceiling, not unlimited sales from a TAM. It caps production economics at the continuing capacity until delayed commissioning, then funds the project, maintenance and receivables/inventory growth. These are analyst assumptions, not order-backed forecasts. The independent ₹4cr solar borrowing is paired with an assumed ₹4cr cash spend and pledged DSRA; a ₹5cr spend sensitivity is retained. No solar savings are invented. The old ₹1.11cr capital advance is neither available cash nor an automatic offset against the new project.\\n\\nReinvestment may be attractive if customers absorb the new output without excessive credit. But the current disclosed customer decline, manufactured-sales slowdown, related-party lease and poor recent collections limit duration confidence. GQS remains NS because growth quality cannot be inferred from profit expansion or capacity announcements alone.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\nFour promoter family members control the business and are material creditors and lessors. FY26 director remuneration was ₹0.8280cr, about 12.77% of PAT and 29.09% of CFO. Four-promoter interest was ₹0.2481cr and family rent ₹0.1760cr. Roopa Engineers related-party sales were ₹0.7340cr and vehicle-hire income ₹0.0752cr. These are disclosed transactions, not proof of diversion; assess terms, recovery and conflicts. The new plot lease from Deval, a director relative, runs December 2025–November 2035 at ₹40,000 monthly with 5% biennial increases. Lease cash expense stays in operating costs under the actual AS presentation. [S01 Annex31 pp192–193, lease disclosures; E11]\\n\\nOwnership must be dated. March promoter holding of 95.23% is not post-IPO ownership. After September transfers and the fresh issue, prospectus promoters hold 6,875,750 shares (62.13%) plus promoter group 175,000 (1.58%), total 63.71%. The theoretical non-promoter 36.29% is not immediately tradable free float: lock-ins and market-maker holdings matter. Current encumbrances could not be independently verified and are ND, not zero. A 6:1 September 2025 bonus adjusts EPS history but creates no economic value. [S01 pp70–73; E13]\\n\\n| Dated commitment/event | Outcome available at cutoff | Judgment |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY24 PPE records under process | FY25/FY26 later CARO says proper records; affirmative IFC | Improvement, not proof of all historical reconciliation |\\n| Auditor resignation 3 July 2025 | Other commitments disclosed; GMCS appointed 21 July | Transition reviewed; no scope-limitation allegation found |\\n| Narol closure 15 December 2025 | Missing factory licence/GPCB approvals; site closed | Actual execution/compliance weakness, not future capacity |\\n| DRHP September 2025 schedule/budget | January 2027 operation shifted to March 2027; project ₹9.8723cr to ₹10.6098cr | Two-month reschedule and 7.47% higher budget; not a missed commissioning deadline yet |\\n| Construction cheque 2 July 2026 | ₹0.20cr paid per prospectus | Payment, not completed asset |\\n| Solar loan by 31 August 2026 | ₹4cr drawn; commissioning/spend reconciliation ND | Debt is real; savings unproven |\\n| March 2027 capacity launch and IPO utilization | Future at cutoff; no verified October order/usage certificate | No delivery credit yet; test delay/overrun |\\n\\nDRHP printed83–84 also provides a real forecast check: projected FY26 inventory ₹537.07 lakh versus actual ₹698.40 lakh (+30.04%); receivables ₹1,287.92 lakh versus ₹1,277.75 lakh (−0.79%); payables ₹244.42 lakh versus ₹475.90 lakh (+94.71%). More supplier funding and inventory than planned offset a broadly met receivable projection. The proposed FY26 IPO working-capital inflow could not occur because the IPO followed FY26. Total-WC/funding definitions differ, so this is a component comparison, not a revenue forecast variance or default finding. [S02 pp83–84; S01 accounts]\\n\\nOriginal audits, CARO, IFC, audit-trail, restatement examination and notes were reviewed, not just the opinion. FY24 was unmodified but proper PPE/intangible records were still being maintained and IFC was exempt. FY25/FY26 were unmodified with affirmative IFC and operational audit trails. FY25 CARO records the auditor resignation; FY26 does not report another. Bank stock-statement differences recurred: ₹106.92 lakh in FY25 and ₹76.07 lakh in FY26, respectively 10.68% and 5.07% of book figures. The auditor's explanation involves chemicals/packing and non-goods liabilities. Obtain corrected borrowing-base reconciliations before upgrading governance confidence. [S04–S08; E06/E08]\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\n| Year | Sales | Manufactured | EBITDA* | EBIT* | PAT | CFO | Capex | Advance | FCF proxy | Debt | Cash |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23 | 34.66 | 33.89 | 2.35 | 1.68 | 0.63 | 3.29 | 2.68 | 0.00 | 0.61 | 10.68 | 0.25 |\\n| FY24 | 35.71 | 35.19 | 4.31 | 3.16 | 1.50 | 5.86 | 2.55 | 0.00 | 3.32 | 8.98 | 0.20 |\\n| FY25 | 45.34 | 39.90 | 8.16 | 6.89 | 4.69 | 3.81 | 1.08 | 0.00 | 2.74 | 7.03 | 0.14 |\\n| FY26 | 50.73 | 42.07 | 10.06 | 8.82 | 6.48 | 2.85 | 1.42 | 1.11 | 1.42 | 7.42 | 0.07 |\\n\\n| Year | D&A | Normalized PAT* | Diluted EPS, bonus basis | Equity (ROE denominator), ₹cr | Value-added proxy |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23 | 0.67 | 0.52 | 0.78 | 3.70 | 9.24 |\\n| FY24 | 1.15 | 1.40 | 1.86 | 5.21 | 11.90 |\\n| FY25 | 1.28 | 4.48 | 5.81 | 9.89 | 16.85 |\\n| FY26 | 1.24 | 6.07 | 8.04 | 16.37 | 20.06 |\\n\\n*Operating EBIT = PBT + full finance cost − all other income; EBITDA adds P&L D&A. FY26 operating EBITDA is ₹10.0636cr versus marketing KPI EBITDA ₹10.0032cr, whose definition excludes other financial charges. Scrap and vehicle rent may recur, so removing all other income is deliberately conservative, not a claim it is all exceptional. Normalized PAT uses (PBT − other income) ×75%; reported PAT remains separate. Subsidies, interest and lower financing cost are not organic revenue growth. Gross profit on a fully comparable industrial basis is ND; the separate value-added proxy excludes labor/power and is not called gross margin. [S01 Annex21–28; `financial_calculations.json`]\\n\\nFY24–26 cumulative CFO/PAT, computed as a ratio of sums, is **98.83%**: ₹12.5240cr / ₹12.6722cr. It is not an average of annual ratios, and is slightly below one. FY26 alone is about 43.90%. The strong FY24 CFO benefited from ₹6.7945cr receivables collection; FY26 absorbed ₹2.7140cr receivables and ₹2.8895cr inventory, with another ₹0.6671cr advances use. Capex does not explain away weak CFO. Deducting FY26 cash capex and capital advance leaves only ₹0.3121cr, before financing-classified interest ₹0.7306cr. [S01 CF/MD&A; E12]\\n\\n| Year | ONWC | IC | IC residual | ROIC avg | ROCE avg | ROE avg | AR days | Inv days | AP days |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23 | 8.58 | 14.13 | 0.0000 | NS | NS | NS | NS | NS | NS |\\n| FY24 | 6.73 | 14.07 | -0.0001 | 16.80% | 22.40% | 33.76% | 106.84 | 51.26 | 90.28 |\\n| FY25 | 9.50 | 16.56 | 0.0000 | 33.72% | 44.96% | 62.07% | 69.54 | 54.96 | 56.89 |\\n| FY26 | 14.82 | 23.10 | 0.0001 | 33.37% | 44.50% | 49.37% | 82.88 | 65.92 | 54.19 |\\n\\nThree-year change in normalized NOPAT 5.36 / change in IC 8.97 = 59.72%. Net cash capex less D&A 1.38 + ONWC 6.24 + capital advances 1.11 + deposits 0.49 − increase in long gratuity 0.25 + residual fixed/disposal/reclassification 0.00 = 8.97. Lagged proxy 175.31% is particularly distorted by its small denominator; not a project return.\\n\\nIC includes capital advances and operating deposits, not only depreciated PPE; the financing-side identity is reconciled with small rounding residuals. Average opening/closing IC is used for ROIC/ROCE; major intra-year changes remain a limitation. Receivable days use average balances and sales as a credit-sales proxy. Inventory/payable days use a disclosed material/trading/finished-inventory cost proxy, excluding labor and power. They are not the issuer's closing-balance budget days. FY23/FY24 interest and capital creditors of ₹0.2485/0.0345cr are excluded from operating working capital and included as financing claims in the IC reconciliation; the FY23 ₹0.0128cr income-tax asset is excluded. Minor rounded FD/provision differences remain visible. No return proxy is an expansion-project IRR.\\n\\n**Original versus restated accounts are retained, not silently overwritten.** Original FY24 CFO ₹5.4391078cr differs materially from restated ₹5.8645cr. Original opening cash ₹0.1000429cr versus restated ₹0.25cr is explained in cash-definition terms by DRHP printed191 Annex18, which lists ₹10.00 lakh cash and ₹15.00 lakh FD with maturity below three months; the complete CFO reclassification bridge was not located. Original FY24 revenue ₹35.7239776cr and PAT ₹1.4402628cr differ from restated ₹35.7053cr/₹1.5036cr. Restatement identifies gratuity, expenses, tax, depreciation and deferred-tax adjustments. FY23 original comparative also differs. FY25 original CFO ₹3.8086cr, FY26 comparative ₹3.8088cr and restated ₹3.8131cr remain distinct. FY26 original CFO/capex/D&A are ₹2.8484/1.4263/1.2395cr versus restated ₹2.8464/1.4243/1.2375cr; closing cash is unchanged. P&L D&A ₹1.2395cr versus cash-flow addback ₹1.2375cr leaves ₹0.0020cr unresolved. [S04/S05/S07/S09; E07]\\n\\nMarch debt was ₹7.4175cr, with ₹3.4002cr promoter loans payable on demand reclassified as current; this is not all new bank funding. By 31 August the prospectus shows debt **₹11.7802cr**, comprising CC ₹3.8236cr against ₹6.25cr sanction, old term ₹1.0914cr, solar ₹4cr and promoter ₹2.8652cr. Solar carries 7.9% interest and 78 principal months after a six-month moratorium. A ₹0.1947cr DSRA and a prior ₹0.0962cr FD are pledged. Note19's no-lien statement conflicts with collateral descriptions, so all March ₹0.2326cr FD is excluded from free cash. The ₹0.0001cr rounded debt-component difference is retained. [S01 finance pp203–207 and annexures; E10]\\n\\nFY26 original Rule11 says no balance-sheet-date pending litigation/contingent liability; its notes/restatement disclose a ₹33.38 lakh GST contingency. The notice arrived 14 July 2026 for FY23 credits and was disputed in August; subsequent-event timing may explain the wording but has not been formally reconciled. A recovery suit for ₹14.82877 lakh has inconsistent transaction-year descriptions. March capital commitments ₹3.4417cr are not assumed additive to or automatically netted against the IPO project without a contract-level bridge. Related parties, bank collateral, land leases, tax/employee provisions and dilution are not omitted from the risk review. [S06 Rule11; S07 note28; S01 Annex30/litigation]\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n| Metric | Value |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| price | 102 |\\n| market_cap | 112.89 |\\n| reported_fy26_PE_postIPO_sharebase | 17.41 |\\n| normalized_fy26_PE_25pct_ex_other_income | 18.60 |\\n| FY26_EPS_postIPO | 5.86 |\\n| price_sales | 2.23 |\\n| PB_March_book_mixed_date | 6.89 |\\n| PB_postIPO_proforma_book_ignores_interim_profit | 3.50 |\\n| current_EV | NS |\\n| current_EV_status | NS: Octobercash/debtunverified; no nettingallIPOasfreecash |\\n| historical_March_net_debt_mixed_date_EV | 120.24 |\\n| historical_mixed_date_EV_EBITDA | 11.95 |\\n| historical_mixed_date_EV_EBIT | 13.63 |\\n| reported_FCF_yield | 1.26% |\\n| cash_after_capex_advance_yield | 0.28% |\\n\\nThe earnings multiple is post-IPO share-base adjusted, not the historical ₹8.04 EPS denominator. Current EV is NS: March cash plus all IPO proceeds minus old debt would misstate project restrictions, fees, new solar debt and interim movements. The mixed-date EV/book cross-checks are labeled and not investment-ready. Historic FCF yields do not forecast post-expansion distributable cash.\\n\\n### Cash-linked scenario design\\n\\nThe model begins with audited 31 March cash ₹0.068cr, not a fabricated IPO-date cash balance. Seven pre-entry periods reconcile disclosed March–August debt amounts, assumed timing/spending, July construction, IPO gross cash/remaining fees and ring-fencing. It settles legacy short-term provisions ₹0.4923cr, comprising tax ₹0.4331cr, current gratuity ₹0.0590cr and CSR ₹0.0002cr; the date is assumed 30 September with an April stress. Conditional entry cash is about ₹8.0043cr plus separate project reserve/DSRA, not an assertion of actual October cash.\\n\\nEvery subsequent month has four dated collection/payment cycles. Early collections can fall before costs, late collections after; receivables, inventories, payables, cash taxes, interest, maintenance, capacity spending and debt principal link to cash. Borrowing-base headroom is a cap, not a commitment. No-optional and bounded-optional versions are separate. Optional bank gross is capped at ₹2.4264cr with 1% fees/13% interest, including at most ₹1cr before entry. Equity is prohibited before entry; later gross is capped at ₹5cr, at ₹66.30 (35% below ₹102), with 4% fees and whole-share ceiling rounded up to meet need, subject to 12,000,000 authorized shares. Share authorization is not issuance approval. Heavy-discount/no-equity/no-bank stresses are retained.\\n\\nBear/base/bull assume manufactured sold-unit growth of 3/12/18%, prices ₹4,800/5,350/5,500, EBITDA margins 10/18/21%, receivable days 130/95/85, inventory days 110/85/70, supplier days 35/52/55, and commissioning delays 12/3/0 months. Project overruns are 25/10/0%; maintenance is 3/2.5/2.5% of sales. The bear calls all promoter debt and half the CC; base calls a quarter of promoter debt. The model uses a conservative 25% cash operating tax without an interest tax shield. Input costs at 65% of sales are an explicit working-capital proxy, not a verified future COGS ratio. Leasing remains in operating costs, with continuation renewal cost embedded; no dividends, NCI, subsidy or speculative asset-recovery credit is added.\\n\\n| Case / funding | Y5 sales | Y5 EBITDA | Bank rescue | Equity gross | End shares | Terminal price | 5Y net IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear/no_optional | 45.95 | 4.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | NS | NS |\\n| bear/bounded_optional | 45.95 | 4.60 | 2.43 | 5.00 | 11821647 | NS | NS |\\n| base/no_optional | 81.11 | 14.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | 80.14 | -5.09% |\\n| base/bounded_optional | 81.11 | 14.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | 80.14 | -5.09% |\\n| bull/no_optional | 102.87 | 21.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | 129.49 | 4.47% |\\n| bull/bounded_optional | 102.87 | 21.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | 129.49 | 4.47% |\\n\\nThe first cash breach is permanent for valuation eligibility. Subsequent rows remain labeled unfunded counterfactuals, not a miraculous cure. The bear without optional funding breaches on **19 March 2027** during operating payments. Finite optional financing delays but does not remove failure: the linked bear exhausts ₹2.4264cr bank gross and ₹4.99999461cr equity gross, issuing 754,147 shares, and cannot repay debt at the horizon. It is not assigned an ordinary price or IRR.\\n\\nA terminal value is earned only after debt and the legacy ₹0.4cr long-gratuity claim are paid, next-year operating cash cycles stay funded, and economic reinvestment is covered. Required capex is the larger of physical maintenance/growth needs and the amount needed to prevent incremental ROIC exceeding 18% at 3% growth. A cash reserve covers the greater of ₹0.05cr, 2.5% next-year sales and the dated peak cash deficit; reserve growth is also deducted. DSRA, deposits and capital advances receive no separate equity asset credit. All business EV is a terminal-continuation value; pre-horizon cash accumulation is added once after claims/reserve, not also distributed as dividends. A 10× next-year EBIT cross-check is retained, not taken from a peer P/E.\\n\\n### Expectations, sensitivities and hurdles\\n\\n| Growth | Margin | Survives? | Net IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| .06 | .14 | True | -15.66% |\\n| .06 | .18 | True | -8.49% |\\n| .06 | .22 | True | -3.29% |\\n| .12 | .14 | True | -12.87% |\\n| .12 | .18 | True | -5.09% |\\n| .12 | .22 | True | 0.42% |\\n| .18 | .14 | True | -10.66% |\\n| .18 | .18 | True | -2.47% |\\n| .18 | .22 | True | 3.26% |\\n\\n| Discount | Terminal growth | Survives? | Net IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| .11 | .01 | True | -1.78% |\\n| .11 | .03 | True | 0.11% |\\n| .11 | .05 | True | 2.87% |\\n| .14 | .01 | True | -5.94% |\\n| .14 | .03 | True | -5.09% |\\n| .14 | .05 | True | -4.00% |\\n| .17 | .01 | True | -8.98% |\\n| .17 | .03 | True | -8.67% |\\n| .17 | .05 | True | -8.35% |\\n\\n| Hurdle | Base conditional entry price |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| 12.00% | 44.57 |\\n| 15.00% | 39.05 |\\n| 18.00% | 34.34 |\\n\\nThese entry prices are assumption-dependent diagnostics, not observable fair value or buy levels. Execution assumes 1% each way; 0/2/5% each-way sensitivity is in the valuation file because actual spread/impact is unknown. Reverse expectations test sold-unit growth from −5% to 40% for both financing policies and each 12/15/18% hurdle. Capacity saturation, cash failure and lack of crossings are retained, not discarded. Where bisection is attempted every trial/end point remains linked; an infeasible midpoint invalidates that bracket rather than proving a crossing. The tested range cannot prove impossibility outside the stated assumptions. 90 unique full paths, 94 original requests, 6 non-surviving paths.\\n\\n### Execution audit\\n\\nA 2,000-share lot costs ₹204,000 at the reference price. Two lots cost ₹408,000; three cost ₹612,000, exceeding the illustrative ₹5 lakh. Fractional lots are not used to make implementation appear feasible. Secondary close/volume rows do not establish actual traded value, bid–ask spread or delivery depth; close×volume is not misreported as turnover. Primary 20-/60-session mean/median, zero-trade counts and current circuit/surveillance history are ND. Pre-listing days are NA, not zeros, and missing BSE data are not inferred from absent NSE trades. The theoretical exit formula uses 2/5/10% participation and the lesser of 60-session mean/median; because the denominator is missing, all exit estimates and L remain NS. Conflicting SM/ST master bands and secondary reports of MT-to-M rolling settlement are not promoted to verified current restrictions. [E15, S16–S19]\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nThe strongest counterargument is that the earnings jump reflects favorable manufacturing spreads, finance-cost relief and a trading-driven revenue mix, not durable demand or pricing power. New capacity then arrives into weak textile orders while receivables and nickel inventory consume cash. A solar-funded asset adds debt without verified savings; promoter demand loans, bank non-renewal and project overruns create a linked financing need. Delayed collections can cause failure before positive year-end earnings are realized.\\n\\n**Conditional impairment risk: High.** The no-rescue bear fails during operations/project ramp; capped rescue can defer but not cure the modeled terminal funding deficit. A failure path has NS shareholder recovery, not a fabricated zero-valued ordinary equity forecast. Dilution and inability to exit can crystallize permanent loss, distinct from a temporary quotation drawdown. Recovery depends on collected receivables, saleable inventory, productive assets and creditor priority; carrying values are not liquidation proceeds. A quoted lower P/E does not resolve those claims.\\n\\nThesis-break variable: available unrestricted cash after dated collections, statutory/employee obligations, project commitments and lender/promoter calls. A negative verified forward cash balance without committed funding, repeated undisclosed use-of-proceeds diversion, or production without cash-paying demand would overturn the expansion thesis. These are conditional triggers, not allegations that they already occurred.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\n**5× / 10× classification: Speculative / unsupported.** No multibagger target is justified. Tenfold price appreciation requires approximately 58.49% annually for five years, 38.95% for seven or 25.89% for ten. Fivefold over five years needs 37.97% annually. Reported sales growth cannot be directly multiplied into per-share returns: manufactured demand, net margin, capital spending, dilution and terminal valuation must all reconcile. The proposed capacity is roughly 2.19× the continuing base; that alone is neither 5× earnings nor 10× equity value. The cash-linked bull fails the ordinary 15% hurdle at the reference price under its stated terminal economics. Further expansion beyond the disclosed project requires additional funded assets and customer demand, not free terminal growth.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\n1. **Cash and funding:** obtain post-IPO bank reconciliation, restrictions, August-to-October debt changes and monthly maturities; warning if available cash cannot cover three months of essential operating/project obligations without an uncommitted rescue.\\n2. **Manufactured demand:** track sold screens, realized price/mix and nickel-adjusted manufacturing revenue separately from trading; FY26 manufactured sales ₹42.0734cr and ~5.45% growth are the baseline.\\n3. **Cash conversion:** FY26 CFO/PAT ~43.90%, three-year ~98.83%; require sustained improvement over two comparable reporting periods, not one collection spike.\\n4. **Receivables/inventory:** March AR ₹12.7775cr, inventory ₹6.9840cr and >6-month AR ₹1.2276cr; investigate worsening age rather than relying solely on total days.\\n5. **Capacity milestone:** independently verify equipment order, installation, approvals and commercial output versus March 2027 plan; >3-month delay or >10% cost overrun triggers a refreshed funding model.\\n6. **Solar:** reconcile the ₹4cr loan to invoice payments, asset commissioning, DSRA and measured generation/savings; no profit credit until supported.\\n7. **Governance/control:** reconcile bank stock statements, original/restated cash flow and GST disclosure chronology; obtain all factory/environmental permissions and RPT terms.\\n8. **Capital/proceeds:** confirm exact issued shares, utilization of ₹9.9046cr project/₹6cr WC allocation, any new options/warrants or fresh equity, promoter encumbrance and creditor calls.\\n9. **Implementation:** obtain primary BSE values/zero sessions/spread/circuits, lock-ins and current lot/restrictions; reassess the actual intended position rather than assuming ₹5 lakh is appropriate.\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Research hold / pending evidence.** No numerical quality or investment rank is assigned. The framework's classification matrix cannot be applied as if NS were a midpoint; compounder criteria are not met because underlying revenue durability, complete BQS/GQS, cash-backed incremental returns and favorable investability gates are not established. No positive investment classification follows from the historic profit rebound. This is not an instruction to sell an existing holding; holdings, taxes and actual exit depth were not supplied.\\n\\nA lower price improves conditional return mathematics but cannot repair data or liquidity holds. Upgrade requires verified sold-volume/price economics, reconciled cash and debt, funded commissioning, adequate post-project working capital and executable trading evidence. A model price alone is insufficient. This record is separate SME research using the same v3 format; it is not merged with main-board rankings and is not itself a publication action.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\n**Evidence ledger:** `audit_evidence.json` contains claim IDs E01–E15, scope/type/confidence and contradictions. `source_register.json` links each source; `historical_inputs.json` preserves original/restated vintages. `peer_comparison.json` and `commitment_outcomes.json` retain named-peer and promises-versus-outcomes detail. `framework_checklist.json` explicitly maps all 15 sections, 13 factors, 4 gates and 16 final checks. The checklist confirms treatment, not that missing facts were found.\\n\\n**Assumptions:** All forward inputs in `model_inputs.json` are A, not management guidance. Growth, prices, margins, cash-cycle timing, pre-entry payments, discretionary financing, capacity ramps, tax, maintenance, cost of capital, terminal growth/ROIC and execution costs are independently visible. Historical V inputs and derived C calculations remain distinct from M expansion guidance. No subjective scenario probabilities were assigned.\\n\\n**Open questions:** FY22 and earlier audits; constant-price sold-volume history and customer retention; complete original-to-restated cash reconciliation; post-IPO cash/utilization; solar spending and commissioning; bank borrowing-base and pledge differences; contract-level capital commitments; GST subsequent-event reconciliation; current promoter encumbrance and complete BSE implementation data. No own-issuer public credit-rating rationale was located. A peer's rating is not Roopa's rating.\\n\\n**Reconstruction:** Standard-library Python regenerates all reports, exact-score calculations, historical financial metrics, every full scenario/reverse path, the all-path cash-event CSV and validation from source-safe authored inputs. Failed paths are preserved and deduplicated by effective assumptions with original request mappings. Compact archives contain the minimum authored inputs/code; full archives are standalone, not deltas. Generated outputs and attestations can be removed and rebuilt against an externally retained hash baseline. Raw third-party PDFs, extracted source text, OCR images, private helper code and private Library identifiers are excluded from both deliverables.\\n\\n### Linked primary and corroborating records\\n\\n- **S01** [Final prospectus](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Roopa-Scree-Limited-Prospectus-2.pdf), 2026-09-29. 321-page offer document; financials printed160–201, finance203–207, objects73–91, business/risks, capital and litigation. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S02** [Draft prospectus](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/DRHP-ROOPA-SCREEN-FINAL.pdf), 2025-09-29. 334-page draft; FY23–25 comparable restated accounts; old projections superseded, not current guidance. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S03** [Annual report FY24](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Annual-Report-Roopa-2023-2024.pdf), FY24. 44-page directors/audit package. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S04** [Original FY24 audit and financials](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/AUDIT-REPORT-2023-2024.pdf), FY24. 34 pages; opinion/CARO/notes and FY23 comparatives, original cash-flow vintage. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S05** [Original FY25 audit and financials](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Audited-FS-Roopa-Screen-Ltd-FY-24-25.pdf), FY25. 31 pages; complete audit/annexures, financials and notes. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S06** [Original FY26 audit report](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Signed-Roopa-Mar-26-Audit-report.pdf), 2026. 9 pages, full opinion/Rule11/CARO/IFC. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S07** [Original FY26 standalone statements](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Signed-Standalone-Financials.pdf), 2026. 22 pages including comparative cash flow and all notes. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S08** [Restatement examination report](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Signed_Roopa_Restated_Examination_Report.pdf), 2026-09-08. 4 pages, distinguishes restatement from original audit. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S09** [Signed restated statements](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Signed-Restated-Financials.pdf), 2026-09-08. 31 pages, FY24–26 restatement, reconciliation and schedules. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S10** [Expansion project report](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/11-Project-Report-Roopa-Screen-L-imited-15-09-2026.pdf), 2026-09-15. 34 pages; prospective estimates, not commissioned capacity. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S11** [Stovec 2025 annual report](https://www.spgprints.com/hubfs/Annaul%20Report_2025.pdf?hsLang=en), CY2025. 144 pages; financials printed92–95 and business/risks; own financial definitions used Status: Read.\\n- **S12** [Stovec June 2026 results](https://www.spgprints.com/hubfs/Results%202nd%20Quarter%202026.pdf?hsLang=en), 2026-08-12. Read for recent trajectory, not merged into Roopa or annualized; no unverified scanned figures used Status: Read.\\n- **S13** [Gurjar rotary screens official product pages](https://www.gurjargroup.com/delta.php), Undated, accessed2026-10-23. Together with https://www.gurjargroup.com/aboutus.php; technology/product breadth only, not independently audited market share Status: Read.\\n- **S14** [Screen-O-Tex official quality and company pages](https://screenotexindia.com/quality/), Undated, accessed2026-10-23. Quality, electroforming and rotary-screen scope; current undated webpage not used as dated operating evidence Status: Read.\\n- **S15** [Infomerics Screen-O-Tex rating rationale](https://infomericstorage.blob.core.windows.net/uploads/pr_Screen_Tex_4sep24_fc49817e21.pdf), 2024-09-04. Original rationale; not a current rating or fully reproduced audit Status: Read.\\n- **S16** [BSE listing notice](https://www.bseindia.com/downloads/UploadDocs/Notices/20260930-45/20260930-45.pdf), 2026-09-30. Primary URL identified but response denied; prospectus and NSE interop security master independently confirm identity/share count/lot Status: Inaccessible, not claimed read.\\n- **S17** [Anand Rathi BSE historical prices](https://anandrathi.com/stocks/roopa-screen-ltd/share-price-history), 2026-10-19 observations. Historical rows read through indexed results; primary turnover/spread/circuit unavailable; price corroboration only Status: Secondary conditional.\\n- **S18** [Trendlyne Roopa Screen quote](https://trendlyne.com/share-price/performance/3674314/15620068/roopa-screen-ltd/), 2026-10-19 15:31. Indexed quote102 and volume92000 corroborate S17; not primary execution evidence Status: Secondary conditional.\\n- **S19** [NSE security master19October2026](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz), 2026-10-19 cached dated security master. Exact ISIN match, shares11067500, lot2000; SM and ST rows retained as conflicting restriction/priceband fields; no NSE trade not proof of no BSE trade Status: Dated cached extract reviewed.\\n\\n### Final quality check and update log\\n\\nThe 16 final checks cover identity/scope/dates; primary V/C/M/A evidence; audit/RPT/funding/dilution; comparable periods; revenue decomposition; ratio-of-sums cash conversion; incremental-capital bridge; normalization/claims; linked cash models; canonical weights; NS/bounds/confidence; permitted overrides; classification; position liquidity; strongest bear; and common-version/tie/unfinished treatment. All are explicitly addressed; missing evidence remains visibly unfinished rather than invented. Research assembled 23 October using information available by 20 October and market data through 19 October only. No prior published score is silently overwritten. Future changes must distinguish new operating evidence, price movements and methodology changes.\\n\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"\\n**Hold: unresolved funding and execution evidence. BQS NS; GQS NS; IAS NS.** Roopa manufactures consumable rotary nickel screens for textile printing, alongside a growing nickel-cathode trading business. The opportunity is replacement demand plus an IPO-funded capacity expansion. The decisive favorable evidence is improved operating profit and utilization at the continuing Sanand factory; the decisive counterevidence is much slower manufactured-sales growth, weak recent cash realization, an old factory closure for missing approvals, and newly expanded debt. At the conditional ₹102 reference price, neither the base nor bull cash-linked diagnostic reaches the 15% equity hurdle. This is a capacity-expansion/cyclical manufacturing thesis, not a verified compounder. Actual post-IPO bank cash and utilization, sold-volume economics, solar-project spending, commissioning and executable liquidity could change the conclusion. Missing evidence is not proof of fraud or insolvency. [S01](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Roopa-Scree-Limited-Prospectus-2.pdf), E02–E15.\\n\\n\"","\"\\nLegal entity **Roopa Screen Limited**, previously Roopa Screen Private Limited; CIN U28112GJ2013PLC074471; **ISIN INE2BTZ01011**, BSE SME **544954 / ROOPA**. Do not confuse it with Roopa Industries. Incorporated 12 April 2013; public conversion 2 June 2025. The research uses standalone Indian GAAP Accounting Standards, not assumed Ind AS. No subsidiaries/JVs or outstanding convertible securities were identified in the reviewed prospectus. [S01, cover/capital/business; E01]\\n\\n₹102 is a secondary-reported BSE close on 19 October, corroborated by Anand Rathi and Trendlyne, not a verified executable bid. Post-IPO issued shares are 11,067,500; conditional market capitalization is **₹112.8885cr**. The primary listing PDF and BSE tape could not be retrieved. The dated NSE interop security master supports identity, share count and 2,000-share lot, but is not a BSE execution record. Horizon: five years to 19 October 2031. Equity hurdle: 15% nominal before personal taxes, with 12%/18% sensitivity. Illustrative position: ₹5 lakh, not a recommended allocation. [E01/E15; S16–S19]\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Resolution needed |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Obtain compliance closure, arm’s-length RPT terms, beneficial ownership and encumbrance verification. |\\n| G2 | Conditional | Reconcile original/restated cash flow, fixed-asset registers and borrowing-base statements. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | Obtain bank statements, drawdowns, purpose utilization, dated maturities, covenants and committed funding. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | Obtain primary traded values, missing/zero-session distinction, lot, spread, circuits and available free float. |\\n\\nG1 is Conditional, not evidenced abuse: family-controlled property/creditor exposure, a licensing failure and reporting differences need scrutiny. G2 is Conditional because later audits are unmodified with affirmative IFC, but the original-to-restated FY24 cash-flow bridge remains incomplete. G3/G4 are Hold, not demonstrated defaults or suspension. An assumed cash shortfall in a stress model does not establish actual issuer insolvency.\\n\\nConfidence is High on exact identity and disclosed capacity/IPO allocation; Medium on restated financials, original-audit interpretation, governance, capital-return proxies and dated debt; Low on constant-price organic demand, actual October available cash, achievable forecast returns and implementation. Overall investment confidence is Low, the weakest material area, not an average. Forecast uncertainty is separate from accurate extraction.\\n\\n\"","\"\\n| Measure | Known K | Coverage | Mathematical raw bound | Final |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 4.04 | 88.00% | ['4.16', '5.24'] | NS |\\n| GQS | 3.30 | 60.00% | ['3.70', '7.30'] | NS |\\n| IAS | 0.00 | 0.00% | ['1.00', '10.00'] | NS |\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | E06,E08,E09,E11,E13 |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | E02,E03,E04,S11,S13,S15 |\\n| REV | NS | Low | E02,E03 |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | E03,E04,E05 |\\n| CAP | 6 | Medium | E10,E12 |\\n| CASH | 5 | Medium | E07,E12 |\\n| BAL | 5 | Medium | E10,E14 |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium | E03,E05,E06,commitment_outcomes.json |\\n| EARN | 6 | Medium | E02,E07,E12 |\\n| R | NS | Low | E14,E15 |\\n| D | NS | Low | E10,E14 |\\n| P | NS | Low | E02,E14,E15 |\\n| L | NS | Low | E15 |\\n\\n| Aggregate | Factor | Weight | Score | Contribution |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | GOV | 0.20 | 4 | 0.80 |\\n| BQS | MOAT | 0.20 | 4 | 0.80 |\\n| BQS | REV | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | DUR | 0.10 | 5 | 0.50 |\\n| BQS | CAP | 0.12 | 6 | 0.72 |\\n| BQS | CASH | 0.10 | 5 | 0.50 |\\n| BQS | BAL | 0.08 | 5 | 0.40 |\\n| BQS | EXEC | 0.08 | 4 | 0.32 |\\n| GQS | REV | 0.40 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | DUR | 0.20 | 5 | 1.00 |\\n| GQS | EARN | 0.15 | 6 | 0.90 |\\n| GQS | CAP | 0.15 | 6 | 0.90 |\\n| GQS | CASH | 0.10 | 5 | 0.50 |\\n| IAS | BQS | 0.25 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | R | 0.35 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | D | 0.20 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | P | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | L | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n\\nAll 13 factors are assigned once and reused. GOV/MOAT/EXEC at 4 reflect documented weaknesses without alleging abuse; DUR/BAL/CASH at 5 reflect mixed evidence; CAP/EARN at 6 reflect historical improvement with important base/capital/cash qualifications. REV is NS because total sales, nickel trading and manufactured rupee sales do not establish constant-price underlying demand. The three-year window was chosen because a five-year comparable series was not located; we did not cherry-pick the faster two-year total-sales period.\\n\\nR is NS rather than mechanically scored 1 from an unsupported base forecast; D/P are NS because present funding and operating assumptions are not established; L is NS because required primary execution data are incomplete. Known-factor confidence is Medium and historical scores are capped at 8 under the short-history convention, although none approaches that ceiling.\\n\\nNo numeric overrides are applied: GOV is not ≤3, no evidenced G1 failure, no proven G3 failure, and one weak cash year does not establish persistent CASH≤3. NS stays NS, never a cap number. The generic IAS bound is [1,10]; propagating the BQS mathematical bound gives a narrower **[1.79,8.81]**, still not a point score or confidence interval. Moving uncertain GOV and CAP by one point together shifts BQS's known contribution by ±0.32 and GQS by ±0.15. No extra risk haircut, undisclosed composite or ranking is permitted. Exact Decimal arithmetic and half-up display rounding are in `score_audit.json`.\\n\\n\"","\"\\nElectroformed rotary nickel screens transfer repeating designs onto textiles. Standard, Delta, Penta and Nova product families vary in mesh/design; durability, uniformity and print quality matter to processors. Screens are recurring consumables, but demand depends on customers' textile throughput and the competition between rotary and digital printing. Nickel cathodes are also traded without material value addition. Trading revenue is not equivalent to manufactured screen demand or proprietary technology. [S01 business/Annex21; E02]\\n\\nFY26 manufacturing contributed ₹42.0734cr, trading ₹8.5650cr and freight ₹0.0888cr. Domestic sales were 99.69%, Gujarat 58.93% and the top five states 96.58%. The top ten customers represented 34.53%, compared with 27.22% in FY25; customers fell to 210 from 228. Top-ten suppliers supplied 96.73%. Penta contributed 38.54% of FY26 sales. Named counterparty disclosures do not demonstrate long-term contracts; firm purchase commitments were not established. Receivables are unsecured; 122.76 lakh was older than six months at March 2026 versus 78.82 lakh a year earlier. [S01 customer/supplier/geography tables, Annex18; E04]\\n\\nThe continuing Sanand unit has 74,400 annual-screen capacity and produced 71,800 in FY26, 96.51% utilization. The closed Narol unit produced another 6,800 before its 15 December 2025 closure. Its 14,400 historical capacity must not be counted as continuing capacity or added again to the new project. Total produced units, 78,600, are not units sold. [S01 manufacturing/risks; E03]\\n\\n\"","\"\\nA plausible advantage is precision electroforming, consistent mesh and customer qualification/service. It is not yet an evidenced strong moat: verified customer retention, lifetime cost advantage, rejection rates, sustained price premium, proprietary IP and independent market-share gains are missing. Product-family names and high utilization alone do not prove pricing power. Nickel volatility, concentrated procurement, textile customer bargaining and substitution by digital printing can weaken economics.\\n\\nThree named primary economic peers challenge the thesis:\\n\\n- **Stovec Industries** is a listed rotary-screen, textile-machinery and digital/graphic-solutions competitor with broader technology and product scope. CY2025 audited revenue fell to ₹198.111cr from ₹234.570cr; PAT fell to ₹6.898cr from ₹12.961cr; CFO was negative ₹1.929cr versus positive ₹16.186cr. Operating EBIT excluding all other income is ₹4.009cr and EBITDA ₹9.765cr; the annual-report highlight uses a different EBITDA definition. These definitions are not mixed. A stronger technology lineage does not guarantee profitable sector growth or cash conversion. Its H1 2026 limited-reviewed report was also read, not annualized. No verified cutoff peer price was obtained, so there is no transplanted current P/E. [S11](https://www.spgprints.com/hubfs/Annaul%20Report_2025.pdf?hsLang=en), [S12](https://www.spgprints.com/hubfs/Results%202nd%20Quarter%202026.pdf?hsLang=en).\\n- **Screen-O-Tex India Private Limited** is a direct rotary-screen/processed-nickel-sheet competitor. Its September 2024 Infomerics rationale cites FY23 revenue ₹76.31cr, EBITDA ₹4.73cr and PAT ₹1.03cr. The historical working-capital cycle was 136 days; facility utilization was 92.6% in July 2024 and March 2024 cash only ₹0.08cr. The historical BB-/Stable/A4 assessment is not represented as its current rating. Promoter quasi-equity treatment also makes credit leverage non-comparable without adjustment. This is contrary evidence against treating screen manufacture as automatically cash-rich. [S15](https://infomericstorage.blob.core.windows.net/uploads/pr_Screen_Tex_4sep24_fc49817e21.pdf), [S14](https://screenotexindia.com/quality/).\\n- **Gurjar Group** markets directly competing precision rotary screens and fine meshes. Its official product pages support competing mechanisms, not independently verified margins or market share. Private peer financials/current valuation remain ND. SPGPrints is Stovec's parent, not a fourth independent competitor. [S13](https://www.gurjargroup.com/delta.php).\\n\\n\"","\"\\n| Interval | Total sales CAGR | Manufactured CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23–FY26, 3 years | 13.53% | 7.47% |\\n| FY24–FY26, 2 years | 19.19% | 9.35% |\\n| 5 / 10 years | ND | ND |\\n\\n| Year | Sales addition | Sales YoY | Manufactured YoY |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY24 | 1.04 | 3.01% | 3.82% |\\n| FY25 | 9.64 | 26.99% | 13.38% |\\n| FY26 | 5.39 | 11.88% | 5.45% |\\n\\nFY23/FY24/FY25/FY26 total revenue was ₹34.6628/35.7053/45.3412/50.7273cr; manufactured revenue ₹33.8943/35.1890/39.8975/42.0734cr. The FY25 addition was disproportionately helped by trading, which rose from ₹0.4596cr to ₹5.3637cr and then ₹8.5650cr. FY26 manufactured-sales growth was only about 5.45% versus 11.88% total-sales growth. [S01 Annex21, S02 Annex20; E02]\\n\\nManufacturing's two-year CAGR is approximately 9.35%, versus approximately 19.20% reported sales. Total output was 73,078/71,127/78,600 screens for FY24/25/26, while the continuing Sanand unit produced 62,038/60,214/71,800. These are production, not invoiced volumes; inventory moved substantially. Dividing revenue by output is a rough plausibility check, not realized selling price. No acquisition-driven growth was identified; price/mix, nickel pass-through and the closure/relocation remain unresolved demand decompositions. Exports were only 0.31% in FY26. Five-/ten-year CAGR and comparable post-FY26 TTM growth are ND, not zero. No single quarter was annualized.\\n\\n\"","\"\\nThe proposed adjacent-site addition of 88,800 screens would raise capacity to 163,200, more than double the continuing base. It is planned, not operating. The ₹10.6098cr project consists of civil work ₹2.9757cr, plant ₹6.7188cr, electrical ₹0.4101cr and contingency ₹0.5052cr. A ₹0.20cr construction cheque is disclosed on 2 July 2026. IPO allocation is ₹9.9046cr with remaining contingency internally funded. The schedule envisages October 2026 ordering, January delivery, February installation/trial and March 2027 commercial production. There was no bank appraisal. [S01 objects; S10; E05]\\n\\nIPO gross proceeds of ₹19.20cr less expenses of ₹3.2773cr yield ₹15.9227cr: project ₹9.9046cr, working capital ₹6.00cr and general corporate purpose ₹0.0181cr. Expense intensity is about 17.07% of gross. March prepaid IPO expense ₹0.5189cr is already outside cash; only the remaining ₹2.7584cr fee is deducted on modeled receipt. The project reserve is ring-fenced and cannot fund ordinary operations, and earmarked working capital is not a dividend. Management's internal-accrual estimates are not bank balances. [S01 objects and Annex28]\\n\\nThe bottom-up model assumes sold-unit bases, 3%/12%/18% growth, explicit unit prices and a 98% capacity ceiling, not unlimited sales from a TAM. It caps production economics at the continuing capacity until delayed commissioning, then funds the project, maintenance and receivables/inventory growth. These are analyst assumptions, not order-backed forecasts. The independent ₹4cr solar borrowing is paired with an assumed ₹4cr cash spend and pledged DSRA; a ₹5cr spend sensitivity is retained. No solar savings are invented. The old ₹1.11cr capital advance is neither available cash nor an automatic offset against the new project.\\n\\nReinvestment may be attractive if customers absorb the new output without excessive credit. But the current disclosed customer decline, manufactured-sales slowdown, related-party lease and poor recent collections limit duration confidence. GQS remains NS because growth quality cannot be inferred from profit expansion or capacity announcements alone.\\n\\n\"","\"\\nFour promoter family members control the business and are material creditors and lessors. FY26 director remuneration was ₹0.8280cr, about 12.77% of PAT and 29.09% of CFO. Four-promoter interest was ₹0.2481cr and family rent ₹0.1760cr. Roopa Engineers related-party sales were ₹0.7340cr and vehicle-hire income ₹0.0752cr. These are disclosed transactions, not proof of diversion; assess terms, recovery and conflicts. The new plot lease from Deval, a director relative, runs December 2025–November 2035 at ₹40,000 monthly with 5% biennial increases. Lease cash expense stays in operating costs under the actual AS presentation. [S01 Annex31 pp192–193, lease disclosures; E11]\\n\\nOwnership must be dated. March promoter holding of 95.23% is not post-IPO ownership. After September transfers and the fresh issue, prospectus promoters hold 6,875,750 shares (62.13%) plus promoter group 175,000 (1.58%), total 63.71%. The theoretical non-promoter 36.29% is not immediately tradable free float: lock-ins and market-maker holdings matter. Current encumbrances could not be independently verified and are ND, not zero. A 6:1 September 2025 bonus adjusts EPS history but creates no economic value. [S01 pp70–73; E13]\\n\\n| Dated commitment/event | Outcome available at cutoff | Judgment |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY24 PPE records under process | FY25/FY26 later CARO says proper records; affirmative IFC | Improvement, not proof of all historical reconciliation |\\n| Auditor resignation 3 July 2025 | Other commitments disclosed; GMCS appointed 21 July | Transition reviewed; no scope-limitation allegation found |\\n| Narol closure 15 December 2025 | Missing factory licence/GPCB approvals; site closed | Actual execution/compliance weakness, not future capacity |\\n| DRHP September 2025 schedule/budget | January 2027 operation shifted to March 2027; project ₹9.8723cr to ₹10.6098cr | Two-month reschedule and 7.47% higher budget; not a missed commissioning deadline yet |\\n| Construction cheque 2 July 2026 | ₹0.20cr paid per prospectus | Payment, not completed asset |\\n| Solar loan by 31 August 2026 | ₹4cr drawn; commissioning/spend reconciliation ND | Debt is real; savings unproven |\\n| March 2027 capacity launch and IPO utilization | Future at cutoff; no verified October order/usage certificate | No delivery credit yet; test delay/overrun |\\n\\nDRHP printed83–84 also provides a real forecast check: projected FY26 inventory ₹537.07 lakh versus actual ₹698.40 lakh (+30.04%); receivables ₹1,287.92 lakh versus ₹1,277.75 lakh (−0.79%); payables ₹244.42 lakh versus ₹475.90 lakh (+94.71%). More supplier funding and inventory than planned offset a broadly met receivable projection. The proposed FY26 IPO working-capital inflow could not occur because the IPO followed FY26. Total-WC/funding definitions differ, so this is a component comparison, not a revenue forecast variance or default finding. [S02 pp83–84; S01 accounts]\\n\\nOriginal audits, CARO, IFC, audit-trail, restatement examination and notes were reviewed, not just the opinion. FY24 was unmodified but proper PPE/intangible records were still being maintained and IFC was exempt. FY25/FY26 were unmodified with affirmative IFC and operational audit trails. FY25 CARO records the auditor resignation; FY26 does not report another. Bank stock-statement differences recurred: ₹106.92 lakh in FY25 and ₹76.07 lakh in FY26, respectively 10.68% and 5.07% of book figures. The auditor's explanation involves chemicals/packing and non-goods liabilities. Obtain corrected borrowing-base reconciliations before upgrading governance confidence. [S04–S08; E06/E08]\\n\\n\"","\"\\n| Year | Sales | Manufactured | EBITDA* | EBIT* | PAT | CFO | Capex | Advance | FCF proxy | Debt | Cash |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23 | 34.66 | 33.89 | 2.35 | 1.68 | 0.63 | 3.29 | 2.68 | 0.00 | 0.61 | 10.68 | 0.25 |\\n| FY24 | 35.71 | 35.19 | 4.31 | 3.16 | 1.50 | 5.86 | 2.55 | 0.00 | 3.32 | 8.98 | 0.20 |\\n| FY25 | 45.34 | 39.90 | 8.16 | 6.89 | 4.69 | 3.81 | 1.08 | 0.00 | 2.74 | 7.03 | 0.14 |\\n| FY26 | 50.73 | 42.07 | 10.06 | 8.82 | 6.48 | 2.85 | 1.42 | 1.11 | 1.42 | 7.42 | 0.07 |\\n\\n| Year | D&A | Normalized PAT* | Diluted EPS, bonus basis | Equity (ROE denominator), ₹cr | Value-added proxy |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23 | 0.67 | 0.52 | 0.78 | 3.70 | 9.24 |\\n| FY24 | 1.15 | 1.40 | 1.86 | 5.21 | 11.90 |\\n| FY25 | 1.28 | 4.48 | 5.81 | 9.89 | 16.85 |\\n| FY26 | 1.24 | 6.07 | 8.04 | 16.37 | 20.06 |\\n\\n*Operating EBIT = PBT + full finance cost − all other income; EBITDA adds P&L D&A. FY26 operating EBITDA is ₹10.0636cr versus marketing KPI EBITDA ₹10.0032cr, whose definition excludes other financial charges. Scrap and vehicle rent may recur, so removing all other income is deliberately conservative, not a claim it is all exceptional. Normalized PAT uses (PBT − other income) ×75%; reported PAT remains separate. Subsidies, interest and lower financing cost are not organic revenue growth. Gross profit on a fully comparable industrial basis is ND; the separate value-added proxy excludes labor/power and is not called gross margin. [S01 Annex21–28; `financial_calculations.json`]\\n\\nFY24–26 cumulative CFO/PAT, computed as a ratio of sums, is **98.83%**: ₹12.5240cr / ₹12.6722cr. It is not an average of annual ratios, and is slightly below one. FY26 alone is about 43.90%. The strong FY24 CFO benefited from ₹6.7945cr receivables collection; FY26 absorbed ₹2.7140cr receivables and ₹2.8895cr inventory, with another ₹0.6671cr advances use. Capex does not explain away weak CFO. Deducting FY26 cash capex and capital advance leaves only ₹0.3121cr, before financing-classified interest ₹0.7306cr. [S01 CF/MD&A; E12]\\n\\n| Year | ONWC | IC | IC residual | ROIC avg | ROCE avg | ROE avg | AR days | Inv days | AP days |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23 | 8.58 | 14.13 | 0.0000 | NS | NS | NS | NS | NS | NS |\\n| FY24 | 6.73 | 14.07 | -0.0001 | 16.80% | 22.40% | 33.76% | 106.84 | 51.26 | 90.28 |\\n| FY25 | 9.50 | 16.56 | 0.0000 | 33.72% | 44.96% | 62.07% | 69.54 | 54.96 | 56.89 |\\n| FY26 | 14.82 | 23.10 | 0.0001 | 33.37% | 44.50% | 49.37% | 82.88 | 65.92 | 54.19 |\\n\\nThree-year change in normalized NOPAT 5.36 / change in IC 8.97 = 59.72%. Net cash capex less D&A 1.38 + ONWC 6.24 + capital advances 1.11 + deposits 0.49 − increase in long gratuity 0.25 + residual fixed/disposal/reclassification 0.00 = 8.97. Lagged proxy 175.31% is particularly distorted by its small denominator; not a project return.\\n\\nIC includes capital advances and operating deposits, not only depreciated PPE; the financing-side identity is reconciled with small rounding residuals. Average opening/closing IC is used for ROIC/ROCE; major intra-year changes remain a limitation. Receivable days use average balances and sales as a credit-sales proxy. Inventory/payable days use a disclosed material/trading/finished-inventory cost proxy, excluding labor and power. They are not the issuer's closing-balance budget days. FY23/FY24 interest and capital creditors of ₹0.2485/0.0345cr are excluded from operating working capital and included as financing claims in the IC reconciliation; the FY23 ₹0.0128cr income-tax asset is excluded. Minor rounded FD/provision differences remain visible. No return proxy is an expansion-project IRR.\\n\\n**Original versus restated accounts are retained, not silently overwritten.** Original FY24 CFO ₹5.4391078cr differs materially from restated ₹5.8645cr. Original opening cash ₹0.1000429cr versus restated ₹0.25cr is explained in cash-definition terms by DRHP printed191 Annex18, which lists ₹10.00 lakh cash and ₹15.00 lakh FD with maturity below three months; the complete CFO reclassification bridge was not located. Original FY24 revenue ₹35.7239776cr and PAT ₹1.4402628cr differ from restated ₹35.7053cr/₹1.5036cr. Restatement identifies gratuity, expenses, tax, depreciation and deferred-tax adjustments. FY23 original comparative also differs. FY25 original CFO ₹3.8086cr, FY26 comparative ₹3.8088cr and restated ₹3.8131cr remain distinct. FY26 original CFO/capex/D&A are ₹2.8484/1.4263/1.2395cr versus restated ₹2.8464/1.4243/1.2375cr; closing cash is unchanged. P&L D&A ₹1.2395cr versus cash-flow addback ₹1.2375cr leaves ₹0.0020cr unresolved. [S04/S05/S07/S09; E07]\\n\\nMarch debt was ₹7.4175cr, with ₹3.4002cr promoter loans payable on demand reclassified as current; this is not all new bank funding. By 31 August the prospectus shows debt **₹11.7802cr**, comprising CC ₹3.8236cr against ₹6.25cr sanction, old term ₹1.0914cr, solar ₹4cr and promoter ₹2.8652cr. Solar carries 7.9% interest and 78 principal months after a six-month moratorium. A ₹0.1947cr DSRA and a prior ₹0.0962cr FD are pledged. Note19's no-lien statement conflicts with collateral descriptions, so all March ₹0.2326cr FD is excluded from free cash. The ₹0.0001cr rounded debt-component difference is retained. [S01 finance pp203–207 and annexures; E10]\\n\\nFY26 original Rule11 says no balance-sheet-date pending litigation/contingent liability; its notes/restatement disclose a ₹33.38 lakh GST contingency. The notice arrived 14 July 2026 for FY23 credits and was disputed in August; subsequent-event timing may explain the wording but has not been formally reconciled. A recovery suit for ₹14.82877 lakh has inconsistent transaction-year descriptions. March capital commitments ₹3.4417cr are not assumed additive to or automatically netted against the IPO project without a contract-level bridge. Related parties, bank collateral, land leases, tax/employee provisions and dilution are not omitted from the risk review. [S06 Rule11; S07 note28; S01 Annex30/litigation]\\n\\n\"","\"\\n| Metric | Value |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| price | 102 |\\n| market_cap | 112.89 |\\n| reported_fy26_PE_postIPO_sharebase | 17.41 |\\n| normalized_fy26_PE_25pct_ex_other_income | 18.60 |\\n| FY26_EPS_postIPO | 5.86 |\\n| price_sales | 2.23 |\\n| PB_March_book_mixed_date | 6.89 |\\n| PB_postIPO_proforma_book_ignores_interim_profit | 3.50 |\\n| current_EV | NS |\\n| current_EV_status | NS: Octobercash/debtunverified; no nettingallIPOasfreecash |\\n| historical_March_net_debt_mixed_date_EV | 120.24 |\\n| historical_mixed_date_EV_EBITDA | 11.95 |\\n| historical_mixed_date_EV_EBIT | 13.63 |\\n| reported_FCF_yield | 1.26% |\\n| cash_after_capex_advance_yield | 0.28% |\\n\\nThe earnings multiple is post-IPO share-base adjusted, not the historical ₹8.04 EPS denominator. Current EV is NS: March cash plus all IPO proceeds minus old debt would misstate project restrictions, fees, new solar debt and interim movements. The mixed-date EV/book cross-checks are labeled and not investment-ready. Historic FCF yields do not forecast post-expansion distributable cash.\\n\\n### Cash-linked scenario design\\n\\nThe model begins with audited 31 March cash ₹0.068cr, not a fabricated IPO-date cash balance. Seven pre-entry periods reconcile disclosed March–August debt amounts, assumed timing/spending, July construction, IPO gross cash/remaining fees and ring-fencing. It settles legacy short-term provisions ₹0.4923cr, comprising tax ₹0.4331cr, current gratuity ₹0.0590cr and CSR ₹0.0002cr; the date is assumed 30 September with an April stress. Conditional entry cash is about ₹8.0043cr plus separate project reserve/DSRA, not an assertion of actual October cash.\\n\\nEvery subsequent month has four dated collection/payment cycles. Early collections can fall before costs, late collections after; receivables, inventories, payables, cash taxes, interest, maintenance, capacity spending and debt principal link to cash. Borrowing-base headroom is a cap, not a commitment. No-optional and bounded-optional versions are separate. Optional bank gross is capped at ₹2.4264cr with 1% fees/13% interest, including at most ₹1cr before entry. Equity is prohibited before entry; later gross is capped at ₹5cr, at ₹66.30 (35% below ₹102), with 4% fees and whole-share ceiling rounded up to meet need, subject to 12,000,000 authorized shares. Share authorization is not issuance approval. Heavy-discount/no-equity/no-bank stresses are retained.\\n\\nBear/base/bull assume manufactured sold-unit growth of 3/12/18%, prices ₹4,800/5,350/5,500, EBITDA margins 10/18/21%, receivable days 130/95/85, inventory days 110/85/70, supplier days 35/52/55, and commissioning delays 12/3/0 months. Project overruns are 25/10/0%; maintenance is 3/2.5/2.5% of sales. The bear calls all promoter debt and half the CC; base calls a quarter of promoter debt. The model uses a conservative 25% cash operating tax without an interest tax shield. Input costs at 65% of sales are an explicit working-capital proxy, not a verified future COGS ratio. Leasing remains in operating costs, with continuation renewal cost embedded; no dividends, NCI, subsidy or speculative asset-recovery credit is added.\\n\\n| Case / funding | Y5 sales | Y5 EBITDA | Bank rescue | Equity gross | End shares | Terminal price | 5Y net IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear/no_optional | 45.95 | 4.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | NS | NS |\\n| bear/bounded_optional | 45.95 | 4.60 | 2.43 | 5.00 | 11821647 | NS | NS |\\n| base/no_optional | 81.11 | 14.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | 80.14 | -5.09% |\\n| base/bounded_optional | 81.11 | 14.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | 80.14 | -5.09% |\\n| bull/no_optional | 102.87 | 21.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | 129.49 | 4.47% |\\n| bull/bounded_optional | 102.87 | 21.60 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 11067500 | 129.49 | 4.47% |\\n\\nThe first cash breach is permanent for valuation eligibility. Subsequent rows remain labeled unfunded counterfactuals, not a miraculous cure. The bear without optional funding breaches on **19 March 2027** during operating payments. Finite optional financing delays but does not remove failure: the linked bear exhausts ₹2.4264cr bank gross and ₹4.99999461cr equity gross, issuing 754,147 shares, and cannot repay debt at the horizon. It is not assigned an ordinary price or IRR.\\n\\nA terminal value is earned only after debt and the legacy ₹0.4cr long-gratuity claim are paid, next-year operating cash cycles stay funded, and economic reinvestment is covered. Required capex is the larger of physical maintenance/growth needs and the amount needed to prevent incremental ROIC exceeding 18% at 3% growth. A cash reserve covers the greater of ₹0.05cr, 2.5% next-year sales and the dated peak cash deficit; reserve growth is also deducted. DSRA, deposits and capital advances receive no separate equity asset credit. All business EV is a terminal-continuation value; pre-horizon cash accumulation is added once after claims/reserve, not also distributed as dividends. A 10× next-year EBIT cross-check is retained, not taken from a peer P/E.\\n\\n### Expectations, sensitivities and hurdles\\n\\n| Growth | Margin | Survives? | Net IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| .06 | .14 | True | -15.66% |\\n| .06 | .18 | True | -8.49% |\\n| .06 | .22 | True | -3.29% |\\n| .12 | .14 | True | -12.87% |\\n| .12 | .18 | True | -5.09% |\\n| .12 | .22 | True | 0.42% |\\n| .18 | .14 | True | -10.66% |\\n| .18 | .18 | True | -2.47% |\\n| .18 | .22 | True | 3.26% |\\n\\n| Discount | Terminal growth | Survives? | Net IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| .11 | .01 | True | -1.78% |\\n| .11 | .03 | True | 0.11% |\\n| .11 | .05 | True | 2.87% |\\n| .14 | .01 | True | -5.94% |\\n| .14 | .03 | True | -5.09% |\\n| .14 | .05 | True | -4.00% |\\n| .17 | .01 | True | -8.98% |\\n| .17 | .03 | True | -8.67% |\\n| .17 | .05 | True | -8.35% |\\n\\n| Hurdle | Base conditional entry price |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| 12.00% | 44.57 |\\n| 15.00% | 39.05 |\\n| 18.00% | 34.34 |\\n\\nThese entry prices are assumption-dependent diagnostics, not observable fair value or buy levels. Execution assumes 1% each way; 0/2/5% each-way sensitivity is in the valuation file because actual spread/impact is unknown. Reverse expectations test sold-unit growth from −5% to 40% for both financing policies and each 12/15/18% hurdle. Capacity saturation, cash failure and lack of crossings are retained, not discarded. Where bisection is attempted every trial/end point remains linked; an infeasible midpoint invalidates that bracket rather than proving a crossing. The tested range cannot prove impossibility outside the stated assumptions. 90 unique full paths, 94 original requests, 6 non-surviving paths.\\n\\n### Execution audit\\n\\nA 2,000-share lot costs ₹204,000 at the reference price. Two lots cost ₹408,000; three cost ₹612,000, exceeding the illustrative ₹5 lakh. Fractional lots are not used to make implementation appear feasible. Secondary close/volume rows do not establish actual traded value, bid–ask spread or delivery depth; close×volume is not misreported as turnover. Primary 20-/60-session mean/median, zero-trade counts and current circuit/surveillance history are ND. Pre-listing days are NA, not zeros, and missing BSE data are not inferred from absent NSE trades. The theoretical exit formula uses 2/5/10% participation and the lesser of 60-session mean/median; because the denominator is missing, all exit estimates and L remain NS. Conflicting SM/ST master bands and secondary reports of MT-to-M rolling settlement are not promoted to verified current restrictions. [E15, S16–S19]\\n\\n\"","\"\\nThe strongest counterargument is that the earnings jump reflects favorable manufacturing spreads, finance-cost relief and a trading-driven revenue mix, not durable demand or pricing power. New capacity then arrives into weak textile orders while receivables and nickel inventory consume cash. A solar-funded asset adds debt without verified savings; promoter demand loans, bank non-renewal and project overruns create a linked financing need. Delayed collections can cause failure before positive year-end earnings are realized.\\n\\n**Conditional impairment risk: High.** The no-rescue bear fails during operations/project ramp; capped rescue can defer but not cure the modeled terminal funding deficit. A failure path has NS shareholder recovery, not a fabricated zero-valued ordinary equity forecast. Dilution and inability to exit can crystallize permanent loss, distinct from a temporary quotation drawdown. Recovery depends on collected receivables, saleable inventory, productive assets and creditor priority; carrying values are not liquidation proceeds. A quoted lower P/E does not resolve those claims.\\n\\nThesis-break variable: available unrestricted cash after dated collections, statutory/employee obligations, project commitments and lender/promoter calls. A negative verified forward cash balance without committed funding, repeated undisclosed use-of-proceeds diversion, or production without cash-paying demand would overturn the expansion thesis. These are conditional triggers, not allegations that they already occurred.\\n\\n\"","\"\\n**5× / 10× classification: Speculative / unsupported.** No multibagger target is justified. Tenfold price appreciation requires approximately 58.49% annually for five years, 38.95% for seven or 25.89% for ten. Fivefold over five years needs 37.97% annually. Reported sales growth cannot be directly multiplied into per-share returns: manufactured demand, net margin, capital spending, dilution and terminal valuation must all reconcile. The proposed capacity is roughly 2.19× the continuing base; that alone is neither 5× earnings nor 10× equity value. The cash-linked bull fails the ordinary 15% hurdle at the reference price under its stated terminal economics. Further expansion beyond the disclosed project requires additional funded assets and customer demand, not free terminal growth.\\n\\n\"","\"\\n1. **Cash and funding:** obtain post-IPO bank reconciliation, restrictions, August-to-October debt changes and monthly maturities; warning if available cash cannot cover three months of essential operating/project obligations without an uncommitted rescue.\\n2. **Manufactured demand:** track sold screens, realized price/mix and nickel-adjusted manufacturing revenue separately from trading; FY26 manufactured sales ₹42.0734cr and ~5.45% growth are the baseline.\\n3. **Cash conversion:** FY26 CFO/PAT ~43.90%, three-year ~98.83%; require sustained improvement over two comparable reporting periods, not one collection spike.\\n4. **Receivables/inventory:** March AR ₹12.7775cr, inventory ₹6.9840cr and >6-month AR ₹1.2276cr; investigate worsening age rather than relying solely on total days.\\n5. **Capacity milestone:** independently verify equipment order, installation, approvals and commercial output versus March 2027 plan; >3-month delay or >10% cost overrun triggers a refreshed funding model.\\n6. **Solar:** reconcile the ₹4cr loan to invoice payments, asset commissioning, DSRA and measured generation/savings; no profit credit until supported.\\n7. **Governance/control:** reconcile bank stock statements, original/restated cash flow and GST disclosure chronology; obtain all factory/environmental permissions and RPT terms.\\n8. **Capital/proceeds:** confirm exact issued shares, utilization of ₹9.9046cr project/₹6cr WC allocation, any new options/warrants or fresh equity, promoter encumbrance and creditor calls.\\n9. **Implementation:** obtain primary BSE values/zero sessions/spread/circuits, lock-ins and current lot/restrictions; reassess the actual intended position rather than assuming ₹5 lakh is appropriate.\\n\\n\"","\"\\n**Research hold / pending evidence.** No numerical quality or investment rank is assigned. The framework's classification matrix cannot be applied as if NS were a midpoint; compounder criteria are not met because underlying revenue durability, complete BQS/GQS, cash-backed incremental returns and favorable investability gates are not established. No positive investment classification follows from the historic profit rebound. This is not an instruction to sell an existing holding; holdings, taxes and actual exit depth were not supplied.\\n\\nA lower price improves conditional return mathematics but cannot repair data or liquidity holds. Upgrade requires verified sold-volume/price economics, reconciled cash and debt, funded commissioning, adequate post-project working capital and executable trading evidence. A model price alone is insufficient. This record is separate SME research using the same v3 format; it is not merged with main-board rankings and is not itself a publication action.\\n\\n\"","\"\\n**Evidence ledger:** `audit_evidence.json` contains claim IDs E01–E15, scope/type/confidence and contradictions. `source_register.json` links each source; `historical_inputs.json` preserves original/restated vintages. `peer_comparison.json` and `commitment_outcomes.json` retain named-peer and promises-versus-outcomes detail. `framework_checklist.json` explicitly maps all 15 sections, 13 factors, 4 gates and 16 final checks. The checklist confirms treatment, not that missing facts were found.\\n\\n**Assumptions:** All forward inputs in `model_inputs.json` are A, not management guidance. Growth, prices, margins, cash-cycle timing, pre-entry payments, discretionary financing, capacity ramps, tax, maintenance, cost of capital, terminal growth/ROIC and execution costs are independently visible. Historical V inputs and derived C calculations remain distinct from M expansion guidance. No subjective scenario probabilities were assigned.\\n\\n**Open questions:** FY22 and earlier audits; constant-price sold-volume history and customer retention; complete original-to-restated cash reconciliation; post-IPO cash/utilization; solar spending and commissioning; bank borrowing-base and pledge differences; contract-level capital commitments; GST subsequent-event reconciliation; current promoter encumbrance and complete BSE implementation data. No own-issuer public credit-rating rationale was located. A peer's rating is not Roopa's rating.\\n\\n**Reconstruction:** Standard-library Python regenerates all reports, exact-score calculations, historical financial metrics, every full scenario/reverse path, the all-path cash-event CSV and validation from source-safe authored inputs. Failed paths are preserved and deduplicated by effective assumptions with original request mappings. Compact archives contain the minimum authored inputs/code; full archives are standalone, not deltas. Generated outputs and attestations can be removed and rebuilt against an externally retained hash baseline. Raw third-party PDFs, extracted source text, OCR images, private helper code and private Library identifiers are excluded from both deliverables.\\n\\n### Linked primary and corroborating records\\n\\n- **S01** [Final prospectus](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Roopa-Scree-Limited-Prospectus-2.pdf), 2026-09-29. 321-page offer document; financials printed160–201, finance203–207, objects73–91, business/risks, capital and litigation. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S02** [Draft prospectus](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/DRHP-ROOPA-SCREEN-FINAL.pdf), 2025-09-29. 334-page draft; FY23–25 comparable restated accounts; old projections superseded, not current guidance. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S03** [Annual report FY24](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Annual-Report-Roopa-2023-2024.pdf), FY24. 44-page directors/audit package. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S04** [Original FY24 audit and financials](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/AUDIT-REPORT-2023-2024.pdf), FY24. 34 pages; opinion/CARO/notes and FY23 comparatives, original cash-flow vintage. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S05** [Original FY25 audit and financials](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Audited-FS-Roopa-Screen-Ltd-FY-24-25.pdf), FY25. 31 pages; complete audit/annexures, financials and notes. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S06** [Original FY26 audit report](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Signed-Roopa-Mar-26-Audit-report.pdf), 2026. 9 pages, full opinion/Rule11/CARO/IFC. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S07** [Original FY26 standalone statements](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Signed-Standalone-Financials.pdf), 2026. 22 pages including comparative cash flow and all notes. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S08** [Restatement examination report](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Signed_Roopa_Restated_Examination_Report.pdf), 2026-09-08. 4 pages, distinguishes restatement from original audit. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S09** [Signed restated statements](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/Signed-Restated-Financials.pdf), 2026-09-08. 31 pages, FY24–26 restatement, reconciliation and schedules. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S10** [Expansion project report](https://www.roopaindia.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/11-Project-Report-Roopa-Screen-L-imited-15-09-2026.pdf), 2026-09-15. 34 pages; prospective estimates, not commissioned capacity. Status: Read; original scans inspected using private local rendering/OCR where needed.\\n- **S11** [Stovec 2025 annual report](https://www.spgprints.com/hubfs/Annaul%20Report_2025.pdf?hsLang=en), CY2025. 144 pages; financials printed92–95 and business/risks; own financial definitions used Status: Read.\\n- **S12** [Stovec June 2026 results](https://www.spgprints.com/hubfs/Results%202nd%20Quarter%202026.pdf?hsLang=en), 2026-08-12. Read for recent trajectory, not merged into Roopa or annualized; no unverified scanned figures used Status: Read.\\n- **S13** [Gurjar rotary screens official product pages](https://www.gurjargroup.com/delta.php), Undated, accessed2026-10-23. Together with https://www.gurjargroup.com/aboutus.php; technology/product breadth only, not independently audited market share Status: Read.\\n- **S14** [Screen-O-Tex official quality and company pages](https://screenotexindia.com/quality/), Undated, accessed2026-10-23. Quality, electroforming and rotary-screen scope; current undated webpage not used as dated operating evidence Status: Read.\\n- **S15** [Infomerics Screen-O-Tex rating rationale](https://infomericstorage.blob.core.windows.net/uploads/pr_Screen_Tex_4sep24_fc49817e21.pdf), 2024-09-04. Original rationale; not a current rating or fully reproduced audit Status: Read.\\n- **S16** [BSE listing notice](https://www.bseindia.com/downloads/UploadDocs/Notices/20260930-45/20260930-45.pdf), 2026-09-30. Primary URL identified but response denied; prospectus and NSE interop security master independently confirm identity/share count/lot Status: Inaccessible, not claimed read.\\n- **S17** [Anand Rathi BSE historical prices](https://anandrathi.com/stocks/roopa-screen-ltd/share-price-history), 2026-10-19 observations. Historical rows read through indexed results; primary turnover/spread/circuit unavailable; price corroboration only Status: Secondary conditional.\\n- **S18** [Trendlyne Roopa Screen quote](https://trendlyne.com/share-price/performance/3674314/15620068/roopa-screen-ltd/), 2026-10-19 15:31. Indexed quote102 and volume92000 corroborate S17; not primary execution evidence Status: Secondary conditional.\\n- **S19** [NSE security master19October2026](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz), 2026-10-19 cached dated security master. Exact ISIN match, shares11067500, lot2000; SM and ST rows retained as conflicting restriction/priceband fields; no NSE trade not proof of no BSE trade Status: Dated cached extract reviewed.\\n\\n### Final quality check and update log\\n\\nThe 16 final checks cover identity/scope/dates; primary V/C/M/A evidence; audit/RPT/funding/dilution; comparable periods; revenue decomposition; ratio-of-sums cash conversion; incremental-capital bridge; normalization/claims; linked cash models; canonical weights; NS/bounds/confidence; permitted overrides; classification; position liquidity; strongest bear; and common-version/tie/unfinished treatment. All are explicitly addressed; missing evidence remains visibly unfinished rather than invented. Research assembled 23 October using information available by 20 October and market data through 19 October only. No prior published score is silently overwritten. Future changes must distinguish new operating evidence, price movements and methodology changes.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"original_heading\"","\"## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\"","4103","\"## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\"","4141","7072","\"## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\"","7096","8646","\"## 4. Business Overview\\n\"","8677","11233","\"## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\"","11262","12666","\"## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\"","12709","14831","\"## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\"","14872","18473","\"## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\"","18499","24550","\"## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\"","24589","32032","\"## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\"","32071","33608","\"## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\"","33636","34430","\"## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\"","34453","36174","\"## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\"","36223","37221","\"## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\"","37266","45601","\"## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\"","\"filename\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"audit_evidence.json\"","\"078ff7fe526046edb32c2fbd8477580f49e2ef199e4ad27553c98d34d8c08731\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"8225e6a16bd1687188bbbb7bd3f33a5cc13fb59c42da5de1bb0a438cd4fac331\"","\"Authored analytical table or source register, not a bundled original filing. Source-access qualifications remain in the research.\"","\"content\"","\"cash_event_ledger.csv\"","\"fa36e1abcf44fe831366218822869fcc851b4bdb7d115d26f4ed8e8b5ae52455\"","\"Exact UTF-8 CSV text\"","\"9d7e97e9fd09a2d62b3c758d0683f5